####################################################################
# cat1b_trigger_count_aggregate.dlog — SHADOW, EXPERIMENTAL. NOT WIRED
# INTO THE PRODUCTION PATH. See cat2a_priority_aggregate.dlog's own header
# for the general rationale (standing AGGREGATE instead of an open-world
# existential search) — same idea, applied to cat1b_asystole_ibp.dlog's
# `FILTER NOT EXISTS { ... }` block.
#
# GROUP KEY: ?ibpFunctionalUnit alone — NO composite (patient, ...) key
# needed here, unlike cat2a/cat2b. Confirmed by tracing mint.py's identity
# resolution: FunctionalUnit particulars are grounded per (patient_id,
# device_id, concept) (the migration plan's Phase 2.5 Item 2 fix), so
# ?ibpFunctionalUnit is already a per-patient-specific IRI, not a shared
# concept-level node the way ?process/?metricType are — the cross-patient
# leak risk that forced a composite key in cat2a/cat2b's shadow rules does
# not apply here.
#
# NO ORDERING REQUIREMENT (unlike cat2a): the arriving alarm's OWN message
# is essentially never triggered by the SAME ibpFunctionalUnit it's
# checking against — it's reached via `?patient isMonitoredBy ?ibpDevice`
# (the patient's separate IBP monitor), not via the arriving alarm's own
# `triggeredBy` device chain. The original NOT-EXISTS query never bothered
# excluding `?otherAlarm != ?alarm` either, for the same structural
# reason. Safe to check AFTER a normal, single-phase insert.
####################################################################

@prefix mda: <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#> .

[?fu, mda:shadowTriggerCount, ?c] :-
    AGGREGATE(
        [?otherMsg, mda:triggeredBy, ?fu] ?g12,
        [?otherAlarm, mda:hasMessage, ?otherMsg] ?g12
        ON ?fu
        BIND COUNT(?otherAlarm) AS ?c
    ) .

# Check shape (validation/shadow_cat1b_aggregate.py): resolve
# ?ibpFunctionalUnit via a one-sided lookup of the arriving alarm's own
# already-inserted data (patient -> isMonitoredBy -> ibpDevice ->
# hasFunctionalUnit), then `FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?ibpFunctionalUnit
# mda:shadowTriggerCount ?anyCount }` — NOT `FILTER(?c = 0)`: an
# AGGREGATE rule only ever produces a fact for a group with at least one
# member, so "no alarm currently triggers this FU" means the aggregate
# fact is ABSENT, never present with value 0. Still a real win over the
# original: the negation now targets a single indexed (subject,
# predicate) lookup on a maintained fact, not a walk of some OTHER
# alarm's own 2-atom chain.
